    {
      "block_id": "p0003-b0005",
      "page_number": 3,
      "reading_order": 34,
      "block_type": "paragraph",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "A day on Earth is actually 23 hours, 56 minutes, not exactly 24 hours, but we round it off to 24 hours because it’s easier to keep track of time.",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 68,
        "source_line_end": 68,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading": "Let's Learn More"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0003-b0006",
      "page_number": 3,
      "reading_order": 35,
      "block_type": "section_heading",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "What is Solstice and When Does It Occur?",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 70,
        "source_line_end": 70,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading_level": 2,
      "heading": "What is Solstice and When Does It Occur?"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0003-b0007",
      "page_number": 3,
      "reading_order": 36,
      "block_type": "paragraph",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "A solstice is a day, when the sun shines vertically over a Tropic (both cancer and Capricorn) in the afternoon and the day is the longest in that hemisphere. On 22 December, the rays of the sun fall directly over the Tropic of Capricorn. This is the longest day in Southern Hemisphere (summer season), while in the Northern Hemisphere, it is the shortest day (winter season). This is called 'Winter Solstice. On 21 June, sunlight falls vertically over the Tropic of Cancer. This is the longest day in Northern Hemisphere and hence it is called 'Summer Solstice', while in the Southern Hemisphere, it is the shortest day.",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 72,
        "source_line_end": 72,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading": "What is Solstice and When Does It Occur?"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0003-b0008",
      "page_number": 3,
      "reading_order": 37,
      "block_type": "section_heading",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Let's Learn More",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 74,
        "source_line_end": 74,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading_level": 2,
      "heading": "Let's Learn More"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0003-b0009",
      "page_number": 3,
      "reading_order": 38,
      "block_type": "paragraph",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Earth keeps rotating due to inertia, the same reason you continue moving forward when you stop a bike suddenly. This movement doesn’t stop because there’s no friction in space to slow it down!",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 76,
        "source_line_end": 76,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading": "Let's Learn More"
    },
    {
